A strict approximable map determines an element of the strict function space whose carrier is the set of strict function tokens it relates. For consistency, the underlying tokens of a finite subset are consistent by the corresponding lemma for approximable maps, and if a subcollection has empty-entailed combined input then that input lies in the bottom element, the input-output union is related, and strictness of the map yields empty entailment of the combined output. For deductive closure, strict entailment supplies function entailment, hence a finite subcollection related by the map together with entailments of input and output; the combined input is consistent by Proposition 2.3, the input-output union is related, and two uses of monotonicity of the relation produce the relation on the entailed token.
